  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Storms possible on Sunday, some may be strong to\nsevere.\n\nA quick moving shortwave embedded within a larger scale trough\nwill swing through the Mid-Atlantic region on Sunday afternoon.\nAhead of this upper disturbance, increasing west/southwest flow\nwill aid in storm organization. Strong daytime heating will\noccur through Sunday, along with increasing boundary layer\nmoisture with dew points rising into the mid/uppers 60s east of\nthe mountains. Should have a corridor of favorable\ndestabilization by the afternoon over portions of the Virginia\nand North Carolina resulting in SBCAPE and MLCAPE exceeding \n1500 J/kg. It appears the most favorable zone for storms will be\neast of the mountains, given a better overlap of shear and \ninstability, however there is a risk of severe storms nearly \nareawide. The main threat will be damaging winds based on model \nsoundings showing inverted-V profiles and DCAPE values also \nexceeding 1000 J/kg. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Above normal temperatures expected through \nSunday.\n\nWhile a more dry airmass pushes into the region today, \ntemperatures this afternoon are expected to be above normal, \nwith highs in the low 90s forecast for the Piedmont, and low to \nmid 80s for mountain locations. The drier airmass will make the \nair feel less muggy today; however, a return of higher \ndewpoints on Sunday combined with increasing high temperatures \nback into the mid 90s for the Piedmont and mid to upper 80s for \nmountain locations, will increase Heat Index values once again \nacross the region. \n\nWhile Heat Advisory criteria will likely not be met,\ntemperatures will be warm across the region, and sensitive\ngroups should avoid outdoor activity during the peak heating of\nthe day. Those that work outdoors should similarly take frequent\nbreaks indoors with AC, and drink plenty of fluids.",
